{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_olaojqkzynxftb5vz7rvfpq4p62l2ut4egs2dku3rv6cfg6cua2q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All 39 tests pass across every phase.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single-function library: `void driver(int x)` printing `x` lines of `\"%d %d\\n\"` with `i` and `j == 2*i`. One source file, one exported symbol, no options, no error returns.\n\n## Findings\n\n**The Rust translation is correct — no divergence found.** `cargo check` was already clean, and the translation faithfully reproduces the C: it calls the platform `printf` (so formatting/buffering are identical rather than reimplemented) and uses `wrapping_add` to match the C build's signed-overflow wrap instead of panicking.\n\nThe one real bug I had to fix was in **my own harness**: my first version redirected the *test process's* fd 1, so libtest's progress output leaked into the captured bytes and produced 14 bogus \"divergences\" (the Rust output was right in every case). I replaced it with `fork()`-per-measurement isolation, which also let me compare the child's **termination status** — so a panic/abort in Rust is caught rather than silently matching.\n\n## Artifacts (in `translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly `driver`; Rust exports `driver`. Symbol diff is empty; 0 unresolved non-libc symbols. No module was left untranslated and nothing is stubbed.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 8 rows. A mechanical grep proved there is *no* error surface: no `return`, `assert`, `NULL`, `errno`, `exit`, error enum, or range constant anywhere (the only `#if` in the tree is the header's include guard). The rows therefore cover the loop guard's rejecting half (`x = 0`, `-1`, `INT_MIN`, randomized negatives), plus explicit rows recording *why* the null-pointer and out-of-range-enum classes are inapplicable, and a no-latched-state row. E5 (`INT_MAX`) is documented as non-executable: it needs ~2.1e9 `printf` calls.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 23 rows. Since there are no flags, the axes are input shape: the decimal-width crossings of `i` and `j` (which happen at *different* `i`, as `j == 2*i`), stdio buffer boundaries, call multiplicity, interleaving, and threading. Includes exhaustive `1..=300` and 700+ seeded random draws.\n- **`verify.sh`** — builds C + both Rust profiles, enumerates Cargo features (there are **none**, so exactly one configuration), and checks symbol parity plus tests per combination.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n1. I tested **both** the release and debug Rust `.so` (the harness asserts it loaded both), since `panic = \"abort\"` and overflow-check settings differ per profile — a `+` instead of `wrapping_add` would diverge only in debug.\n2. I **mutation-tested the harness** to prove it isn't vacuously passing: wrong increment, off-by-one loop bound, altered format string, and a removed `#[no_mangle]` were all detected, and `src/lib.rs` was restored.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified; only the required `c_src/build/` directory was added. Note that `cargo` needs `--offline` here, as crates.io is blocked by the sandbox proxy (`libloading` 0.8.9 was already in the local cache)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":990,"cache_read_input_tokens":82471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":990,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e82102e2-9b4d-4d17-8b91-d97408212f6a","uuid":"7a31db88-601b-4a65-aec7-263928b8241b","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:40:19.112Z"}
